Z||000000||Old Lar||03-03-2001||03:17 PM||||After reading all the problems with everyones Fiero, I'll tell my story.<P>I dug my 87 out of the snow bank, charged the battery, climbed into the passenger compartment (no seats), put the key in and.... it started immediatly.  A little rough at first, the codes erased from a dead battery.  The idle smoothed out to 700-800 rpm and the temp came to 190 and sat there.<P>So since this went so well, I figured I'd replace the drivers seat.  The passenger side of the car is still under snow.  I dropped the driver's seat on the four studs and the holes and studs matched perfectly.  I hand tightened the nuts over the seat bracket, then eased a 13mm socket over the nut and tighted.  The car was still ideling smoothly, rpms still stable, oil pressure at about 70, temp still 190 degrees. I mounted the rear licence plate and wouldn't you know it I dropped one of the licence plate bolts into the snow, but as luck would have it, I reached into the snow and found it.<P>I'm thinking that is this the same type of car I read about in this forum? I mean it started after 4+ months of outdoor storage refrigerated under the snow, no rough idle, no leaks.  Maybe tomorrow, if it stays clear, I can finish digging the car out of the snow, put the passenger seat in and take it out on the road.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It was real nice to hear again, the rumble of the 2.8 V6 of my Fiero.||||reg||12
Z||000001||gabriel||03-03-2001||03:21 PM||||&gt;I mean it started after 4+ months of outdoor storage refrigerated under the snow, <P>Refridgeration works wonders ...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
